Chapter: AccumulatorCorpus
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 THE KILL-RATE CORPUS for Cost Model 3.3, section 5 question 2. It exists
 before the check, and the reason it has to is that a check's kill rate is only
 a number if the corpus already knows the answers.

 EVERY LABEL HERE IS MEASURED, NOT DECLARED. Each entry runs at n, 2n and 4n
 and reports bytes retained, taken from `__heap-save` either side of the call.
 Quadratic allocation scales as n squared, so quadrupling n multiplies bytes by
 about sixteen; linear multiplies by about four. `verdict` divides and thresholds
 at eight, which is a wide gap because the two populations are 4 and 16 rather
 than anything adjacent. A hand-labelled corpus would be an assertion with no
 runner, and it would be graded by the same judgement that wrote it.

 THE NEGATIVES ARE THE HALF THAT MATTERS. A corpus of quadratic cases alone
 cannot tell a good check from one that refuses every append, and that check
 scores a perfect kill rate. Entries 6 to 10 are linear and MUST NOT be flagged.
 Three of them are written to look exactly like the positives to any test that
 reads for `acc &`: `n-fixed-appends` appends to a growing accumulator but a
 constant number of times, `n-fresh-not-acc` appends to something that is not
 the accumulator, and `n-append-empty` appends to the accumulator with a list
 that is always empty. A static filter over the append operator flags all three.

 The numbers are bytes RETAINED, not bytes touched. Bare metal has no collector,
 so what a loop allocates and abandons is exactly what is retained until the
 producing function returns, which is what makes `__heap-save` an honest
 instrument here rather than an approximation.

Section: The Instrument

  bytes-of : Integer -> Integer
  bytes-of (marker) = marker

  verdict : Integer, Integer -> Text
  verdict (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n <= 0 then "degenerate"
    else if at-4n / at-n >= 8 then "quadratic"
    else "linear"

 `keep` is what stops the measurement being optimised into nothing. Each entry
 folds its result into an integer the caller prints, so the allocation cannot be
 dead code. It is summed rather than discarded for the same reason.

  keep : List Integer -> Integer
  keep (xs) = list-length xs

  keep-text : Text -> Integer
  keep-text (t) = text-length t

Section: Positives -- the check MUST catch these

 1. The canonical shape: a self-tail-call whose accumulator is appended to.
    This is the form all three of the 2026-08-14 defects took.

  p-append-one : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  p-append-one (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-append-one n (i + 1) (acc & [i])

 2. The same shape over Text rather than List, because the cost model's 3.1
    table is about Text and the append operator is the same one.

  p-append-text : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  p-append-text (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-append-text n (i + 1) (acc & "x")

 2b. The pair for entry 2, and the one that makes the Text half mean
    something. Same accumulator, same operator, same self tail call; the only
    difference is that the right operand ALLOCATES. Since COMPILER-8 (main
    16039) a Text accumulator extends in place while it stays topmost, so
    entry 2 is linear and this one is not: `show` allocates between the
    appends and pushes the accumulator off the frontier, and the copy comes
    back. This is the shape `unpack-text` had, which is the defect the whole
    cost model was opened for.

  p-append-show : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  p-append-show (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-append-show n (i + 1) (acc & show i)

 3. Appending a constant-size LIST rather than a single element. Same class,
    and it is the form that reads most like ordinary concatenation.

  p-append-chunk : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  p-append-chunk (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-append-chunk n (i + 1) (acc & [i, i, i, i])

 4. `pb-expand-blocks` as it was written before it was fixed. This is the one
    REAL measured instance in the corpus with a published before and after:
    the chapter's own prose records that at the default 4096 blocks this form
    needed between two and three gigabytes and died silently below that, with
    exit zero and truncated output, and that `list-push` fixed it. Entry 7 is
    the fixed form, so the pair is a control on the corpus itself.

  p-expand-blocks-old : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  p-expand-blocks-old (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-expand-blocks-old n (i + 1) (acc & [i, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0])

 5. The shape of `syslog-decode-bytes`, which the format census records as a
    latent instance: a body accumulated with `acc &` under a wire-supplied
    length. Latent because `syslog-parse` has no production caller.

  p-syslog-body : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  p-syslog-body (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else p-syslog-body n (i + 1) (acc & [i - (i / 256) * 256])

Section: Negatives -- the check MUST NOT flag these

 6. The fix for entry 1. `list-push` writes in place while the list is under
    capacity and doubles when it is not, so the array costs its own size.

  n-push-one : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  n-push-one (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else n-push-one n (i + 1) (list-push acc i)

 7. The fix for entry 4, which is what `pb-push-block` actually does today.

  n-push-block : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  n-push-block (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else n-push-block n (i + 1) (n-push-eight acc i 0)

  n-push-eight : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  n-push-eight (acc) (v) (k) =
    if k >= 8 then acc
    else n-push-eight (list-push acc v) v (k + 1)

 8. HARD NEGATIVE. The accumulator is appended to, in a self-tail-call, with
    the append operator, and the cost is linear in n because the append runs a
    fixed FOUR times however large n gets. Any test that reads for the operator
    flags this.

  n-fixed-appends : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  n-fixed-appends (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if i >= 4 then n-fixed-appends n (i + 1) acc
    else n-fixed-appends n (i + 1) (acc & [i])

 9. HARD NEGATIVE. An append per iteration, to something that is NOT the
    accumulator: the appended-to value is rebuilt small every time, so nothing
    grows across iterations.

  n-fresh-not-acc : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  n-fresh-not-acc (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let fresh = [i] & [i + 1]
    in n-fresh-not-acc n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at fresh 0))

 10. HARD NEGATIVE, and the narrowest of the three. The accumulator IS the
     appended-to value and the append IS per-iteration, but the right operand
     is always empty, so the result aliases and nothing is copied. It is a
     silly function; it is here because it is the case a syntactic rule gets
     wrong while an allocation-aware one does not.

  n-append-empty : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  n-append-empty (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else n-append-empty n (i + 1) (acc & [])

Section: Measurement

  m-p-append-one : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-append-one (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (p-append-one n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-p-append-text : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-append-text (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (p-append-text n 0 "")
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-p-append-show : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-append-show (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (p-append-show n 0 "")
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-p-append-chunk : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-append-chunk (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (p-append-chunk n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-p-expand-blocks-old : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-expand-blocks-old (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (p-expand-blocks-old n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-p-syslog-body : Integer -> Integer
  m-p-syslog-body (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (p-syslog-body n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-n-push-one : Integer -> Integer
  m-n-push-one (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (n-push-one n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-n-push-block : Integer -> Integer
  m-n-push-block (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (n-push-block n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-n-fixed-appends : Integer -> Integer
  m-n-fixed-appends (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (n-fixed-appends n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-n-fresh-not-acc : Integer -> Integer
  m-n-fresh-not-acc (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (n-fresh-not-acc n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-n-append-empty : Integer -> Integer
  m-n-append-empty (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (n-append-empty n 0 [])
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Entry

 The base n is 64. Small on purpose: the positives are quadratic, so the point
 separates long before the numbers get large, and a corpus that takes minutes
 to run is one nobody runs.

  base : Integer = 64

 The ratio is printed and not only the verdict. A row that says "quadratic" and
 nothing else hides how close it came to the threshold, and the margin is the
 number that tells a later reader whether 8 is still the right cut. Reported in
 tenths, integer arithmetic throughout.

  ratio-tenths : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ratio-tenths (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n <= 0 then 0
    else (at-4n * 10) / at-n

  show-tenths : Integer -> Text
  show-tenths (t) = show (t / 10) & "." & show (t - (t / 10) * 10)

  row : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (name) (at-n) (at-2n) (at-4n) =
    name & " n=" & show at-n & " 2n=" & show at-2n & " 4n=" & show at-4n
      & " x" & show-tenths (ratio-tenths at-n at-4n) & " " & verdict at-n at-4n

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (row "p-append-one       " (m-p-append-one base) (m-p-append-one (base * 2)) (m-p-append-one (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "p-append-text      " (m-p-append-text base) (m-p-append-text (base * 2)) (m-p-append-text (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "p-append-show      " (m-p-append-show base) (m-p-append-show (base * 2)) (m-p-append-show (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "p-append-chunk     " (m-p-append-chunk base) (m-p-append-chunk (base * 2)) (m-p-append-chunk (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "p-expand-blocks-old" (m-p-expand-blocks-old base) (m-p-expand-blocks-old (base * 2)) (m-p-expand-blocks-old (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "p-syslog-body      " (m-p-syslog-body base) (m-p-syslog-body (base * 2)) (m-p-syslog-body (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "n-push-one         " (m-n-push-one base) (m-n-push-one (base * 2)) (m-n-push-one (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "n-push-block       " (m-n-push-block base) (m-n-push-block (base * 2)) (m-n-push-block (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "n-fixed-appends    " (m-n-fixed-appends base) (m-n-fixed-appends (base * 2)) (m-n-fixed-appends (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "n-fresh-not-acc    " (m-n-fresh-not-acc base) (m-n-fresh-not-acc (base * 2)) (m-n-fresh-not-acc (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "n-append-empty     " (m-n-append-empty base) (m-n-append-empty (base * 2)) (m-n-append-empty (base * 4)))
  end
